/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Ferrari 296 GT Modificata — dealer landing page
   Grammar: filmic one-shot. Every act is a screen, every screen is a shot.

   The first version of this page set the frames as inset plates on a
   sampled ground — a dossier. It was rejected: the page has to be cinema.
   The plate version is kept beside this file as gtm.dossier.css.bak, with
   its markup as _main.dossier.html.bak, because the client may come back
   to the record-and-plate treatment for a spec page.

   What that changes, concretely:
   · a frame is no longer a rectangle on a page — it IS the page. Every
     photograph is full-bleed and owns a whole screen;
   · type lives inside the picture, in regions measured dark enough to
     carry it, lifted by a scrim built as a directional gradient rather
     than a flat wash;
   · reading is never done over a moving picture: every paragraph sits on
     its own quiet band between two shots.

   The photographs are a single studio session — one hall, one light rig,
   a wet floor lit petrol. The page keeps that hall unbroken: the bands
   between shots are the hall's own dark, sampled from the frames' edges.
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

:root {
  /* Ferrari's own ground and nothing sampled from anywhere else. Their
     site is one black, and its sections are separated by imagery and
     interval rather than by tint — so the page is black, the pictures
     carry the colour, and no blue-grey of my own enters the palette. */
  --hall:      #000000;

  --ink:       #FFFFFF;
  --ink-80:    rgb(255 255 255 / .82);
  --ink-60:    rgb(255 255 255 / .64);
  --rule:      rgb(255 255 255 / .22);
  --rule-fine: rgb(255 255 255 / .10);

  /* Two values of one red, each measured into the role it serves — this
     is not a preference and it is not a mistake left in.

     FILLS take ferrari.com's own red, #DA291C, with a WHITE label. That
     is what their site ships: their primary button is rgb(218,41,28),
     white label, 16px regular, square, 57px tall. White on it measures
     4.87:1 and clears AA; white on the Brand Book's brighter #ED1C24
     would be 4.38:1 and fail, which is why a black label was tried here
     and was wrong — Ferrari does not set black type on red.

     RED TEXT takes the Brand Book value, #ED1C24 (p104, Pantone 485).
     The marque's chapter marker is 12px, and at that size the darker
     #DA291C measures 4.31:1 on black — under the floor. ferrari.com ships
     it anyway; this page does not get to.

     Giallo Modena (#FFF200) is a Ferrari colour too, and on this page it
     stays where it belongs: on the car. */
  --accent:      #DA291C;   /* fills */
  --accent-ink:  #FFFFFF;
  --rosso-corsa: #ED1C24;   /* red type — the chapter marker inherits it */

  --measure: 1280px;
  --gutter:  clamp(20px, 4vw, 64px);

  --s-1: 4px;  --s-2: 8px;  --s-3: 12px; --s-4: 16px; --s-5: 20px;
  --s-6: 24px; --s-7: 32px; --s-8: 40px; --s-9: 56px; --s-10: 80px; --s-11: 120px;

  /* type — one family, four sizes. The display is the page's voice and it
     is set big, because a shot needs a title, not a label. */
  --t-hero:    clamp(1.75rem, 2.5vw, 2.5rem);   /* 40px at 1440 */
  --t-display: clamp(1.5rem, 2.2vw, 2.25rem);  /* 36px at 1440, as theirs */
  --t-value:   clamp(1.5rem, 1.9vw, 1.875rem);
  --t-read:    clamp(1rem, .28vw + .93rem, 1.125rem);
  --t-reg:     .875rem;

  --dur: .15s;
  --ease-out: cubic-bezier(.22, .61, .36, 1);
}

body { background: var(--hall); color: var(--ink); font-family: var(--font); font-synthesis: none; -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased; }
.gtm { overflow-x: clip; }

/* ═══ a shot ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   One screen, one photograph, edge to edge. The picture is not a block in
   the layout — it is the ground the type stands on. */
/* Nine screens of exactly one viewport each is a slideshow. The heights
   are now ranked: the peak takes the most, the two quiet acts take the
   least, and the hero and the close hold a full screen because they
   open and close the page. */
/* Their sections set the text at the TOP of the frame, left, with the
   picture running full-bleed under it; the hero is the exception and
   carries its title low. */
.shot {
  position: relative;
  min-height: var(--shot-h, 92svh);
  display: grid;
  align-items: start;
  overflow: clip;
  isolation: isolate;
}
/* -7% threw away 22% of every frame's width at 1440 and 57% at 768. -3%
   is enough travel for the parallax and keeps the subject inside the
   frame down to the phone. */
.shot__media { position: absolute; inset: -3% 0; z-index: 0; overflow: hidden; }
/* Every frame fills the screen, edge to edge, at every width. The crop is
   authored per shot by its own object-position, chosen so the subject
   stands clear of the side the words are on — the type is never asked to
   sit on the car. The media box is taller than the screen so the picture
   can travel against the scroll without ever showing its edge. */
.shot__media img {
  width: 100%; height: 100%;
  object-fit: cover;
  object-position: var(--pos, 50% 50%);
  /* the camera: it closes on the object as the shot arrives, and the
     picture travels against the scroll the whole time it is on screen.
     One idea — a camera move — expressed as two rates. */
  transform: translateY(calc(var(--par, 0) * 1%)) scale(var(--zoom, 1));
  transform-origin: var(--pos, 50% 50%);
  will-change: transform;
}
/* The lift used to be a 95%-black horizontal wash plus a floor, and
   together with the section-foot wipe below it was deleting 22-40% of
   every frame's light and rendering 52-80% of each "full-screen
   photograph" as flat black — the wet studio floor, the best thing in
   this asset set, gone on all nine. Legibility is now bought by putting
   the type where the picture is already dark, and the lift only deepens
   that region rather than manufacturing it. */
.shot__lift {
  position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: 1; pointer-events: none;
  /* Measured off the frames themselves: the bottom 22% of every shot —
     the studio floor — runs at luminance 0.05-0.15 in seven of the nine,
     and 0.54 at its worst (the seats). Type lives in that strip and the
     lift only deepens it. Nothing washes across the car any more, so the
     top two thirds of every photograph are the photograph. */
  background:
    linear-gradient(to bottom, rgb(0 0 0 / .46) 0%, rgb(0 0 0 / .26) 14%,
      rgb(0 0 0 / .10) 26%, rgb(0 0 0 / 0) 40%),
    linear-gradient(to top, rgb(0 0 0 / .70) 0%, rgb(0 0 0 / .42) 10%,
      rgb(0 0 0 / .18) 20%, rgb(0 0 0 / .04) 30%, rgb(0 0 0 / 0) 42%);
}
/* left-set and right-set shots take the same lift: the strip is the
   floor, and the floor runs the width of the frame */
/* the shot hands over to the band below it — the hall closing back in */
.shot::after {
  content: ""; position: absolute; inset-inline: 0; bottom: 0; height: 22svh; z-index: 1;
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgb(0 0 0 / 0), var(--hall));
  pointer-events: none;
}
/* A pool of shadow under the words rather than a wash across the picture.
   It is anchored to the side the type stands on, sized to the column, and
   gone by mid-frame — so the car and the floor keep their light while the
   type gets the ground it needs. The four screens that still measured
   under AA after the crops were chosen by light are exactly the four this
   fixes, without taking anything off the other five. */
.shot__type::before {
  content: ""; position: absolute; inset: -6vh -4rem;
  z-index: -1; pointer-events: none;
  background: radial-gradient(54% 130% at 16% 50%,
    rgb(0 0 0 / .86) 0%, rgb(0 0 0 / .70) 32%, rgb(0 0 0 / .34) 58%, rgb(0 0 0 / 0) 82%);
}
.shot--right .shot__type::before {
  background: radial-gradient(54% 130% at 84% 50%,
    rgb(0 0 0 / .86) 0%, rgb(0 0 0 / .70) 32%, rgb(0 0 0 / .34) 58%, rgb(0 0 0 / 0) 82%);
}
.shot--close .shot__type::before { display: none; }

.shot__type {
  position: relative; z-index: 2; isolation: isolate;
  width: min(100%, var(--measure));
  margin-inline: auto;
  padding-inline: var(--gutter);
  padding-block-start: clamp(7rem, 16vh, 11rem);
}
.shot--hero, .shot--close { align-items: end; }
.shot--hero .shot__type { padding-block: 0 clamp(1.5rem, 3.5vh, 2.5rem); }
/* a right-set shot moves the whole block, not just the ragging: the type
   stands in the margin on that side of the picture */
.shot--right .shot__type { text-align: start; }
.shot--right .shot__type > * { margin-inline-start: auto; }
.shot--right .shot__type > *,
.shot--right .shot__type { text-align: start; }
/* the column has to contain MODIFICATA at the hero size — at 24ch the H1
   overflowed its own max-width by 21px and text-wrap could do nothing */
.shot__title { margin: 0; max-width: 20ch; }
/* the register voice is lifted over a photograph: at 64% ink the engine
   shot's record measured 4.40:1 against its own background, which is under
   the floor. 78% puts it at 5.8:1 and changes nothing on the flat bands. */
.shot__type .reg, .shot__type .facts { color: rgb(255 255 255 / .78); }
/* the hero block sits lower, inside the frame's own dark floor: measured
   at the old height its title crossed the car's lit nose at 2.87:1 */
.shot--hero { --shot-h: 100svh; }
.shot--hero .shot__type { padding-block-end: clamp(1.5rem, 3.5vh, 2.5rem); }
.shot--hero .shot__sub { margin-block-start: var(--s-4); }
.shot--hero .facts--row { margin-block-start: var(--s-5); padding-block-start: var(--s-3); }
.shot--hero .route { margin-block-start: var(--s-5); }
.shot--peak { --shot-h: 118svh; }
.shot--quiet { --shot-h: 84svh; }
/* The close carries the most type on the page — eyebrow, headline,
   paragraph, control, note — and over the rear three-quarter's silver it
   measured 1.67:1. No ramp deep enough to fix that leaves a photograph
   underneath, so the close is composed in two parts at every width, the
   way the phone already had it: the shot plays above, the words stand on
   the page's own black beneath. */
.shot--close { --shot-h: 0; min-height: 0; display: block; }
.shot--close .shot__media { position: relative; inset: auto; height: 62svh; }
.shot--close .shot__lift {
  background: linear-gradient(to top, rgb(0 0 0 / .85) 0%, rgb(0 0 0 / .22) 22%, rgb(0 0 0 / 0) 44%);
}
.shot--close .shot__type {
  padding-block: clamp(3rem, 7vh, 5rem) clamp(4rem, 9vh, 7rem);
  background: var(--hall);
}
.shot--hero .shot__title { max-width: 16ch; }
/* the peak is the only frame in the set that is not the car, and it is
   the one screen that gets the page's largest type */
.shot--peak .shot__title { font-size: var(--t-hero); }
.shot__type > * { max-width: min(24ch, 29vw); }
/* a control is sized by its label, never by the reading measure */
.shot__type > p:has(.btn), .shot__type .btn { max-width: none; width: max-content; }
.shot__sub, .shot__type .read { max-width: min(30ch, 29vw); }
.shot__type .facts { max-width: min(34ch, 29vw); }
.shot__sub {
  margin-block-start: var(--s-5);
  max-width: min(42ch, 40vw) !important;
  font-size: var(--t-read);
  line-height: 1.5;
  color: var(--ink-80);
}
.shot--right .shot__sub { margin-inline-start: auto; }

/* ═══ type ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
/* Measured off ferrari.com's own 296 GT Modificata page: their headlines
   are 36px, weight 400, sentence case, no tracking — "Ferrari 296 GT
   Modificata", "Beyond the limits", "730 hp V6" — and their body runs
   13px/20.8. Ours was uppercase, 44px, tracked and at weight 500: louder
   than the marque is, which is most of why the page read as shouty rather
   than as theirs. Body stays at 16-20px because 13px is under this
   system's own functional floor. */
.hero-type, .display {
  margin: 0;
  font-weight: 400;
  line-height: 1.12;
  letter-spacing: normal;
  text-transform: none;
  text-wrap: balance;
}
.hero-type { font-size: var(--t-hero); }
.display   { font-size: var(--t-display); }
.read {
  margin: 0; max-width: 62ch;
  font-size: var(--t-read); line-height: 1.6;
  color: var(--ink-80); text-wrap: pretty;
}
.read + .read { margin-block-start: 2em; }   /* FNA: paragraph spacing = 2× the font size */
.reg {
  font-size: var(--t-reg); line-height: 1.3; font-weight: 400;
  letter-spacing: .14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-60);
}
.value {
  color: var(--ink);
  /* the register voice's 0.14em is inherited as a computed 1.96px length,
     which at 34px is loose rather than tracked */
  letter-spacing: normal;
  font-size: var(--t-value); font-weight: 500; line-height: 1;
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums lining-nums;
  font-feature-settings: "tnum" 1, "lnum" 1;
}

/* ═══ a band — where reading happens ════════════════════════════════════
   Never over a photograph, never while anything moves. The hall's own
   dark, the measure, and air. */
/* base.css declares `.band { padding-inline: var(--band-pad) }` — 192px
   here — because on the Purosangue page `.band` WAS the content column.
   This page puts its own shell inside it, so that padding was landing on
   top of the shell's and the page ended up with four left edges: dealer
   lockup 64, shot type 130, marker 212, prose 250. Zeroed, three of the
   four collapse into one. */
.band {
  background: var(--hall);
  padding-inline: 0;
  padding-block: clamp(4.5rem, 10vw, 9rem);
}
/* and the marker hangs on the Purosangue column's offset, which does not
   exist here */
:root { --marker-out: 0px; }
/* There is no raised band. Ferrari's own site is one ground and separates
   its sections by imagery and interval; a lifted panel here also put the
   chapter marker's 12px red on #141414, which measures 4.20:1 and is under
   AA. One black, and the marker reads 4.79:1 everywhere. */
.band__in {
  width: min(100%, var(--measure));
  margin-inline: auto;
  padding-inline: var(--gutter);
}
/* A band carries the argument, and — where the deck supplies numbers —
   the record that proves it. The record used to sit on the photograph,
   where it needed a black wash to be read at all; on the page's own black
   it measures better than 15:1 and the band stops being two paragraphs in
   an empty field. */
.band__in:has(.plate) {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 4fr) minmax(0, 7fr);
  gap: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 5rem);
  align-items: start;
}
.band__in:has(.plate) .marker { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
.plate .facts { margin-block-start: 0; max-width: none; }
.plate .facts > div { padding-block: var(--s-4); }
.prose { max-width: 62ch; }
/* one size for body text on the whole page: the bands used to run 22px
   against the shots' 20px and the sheet's 17px — one role, three sizes */

/* Section labels are the Purosangue page's own marker — the marque's
   Chapter component, inherited from base.css rather than re-drawn here:
   number, a fixed 96px rule, name, 12/15 in rosso corsa. The only thing
   this page adds is the interval under it. */
/* Ferrari sets this component at 12px and ferrari.com ships it that way;
   this page holds to the 14px floor for functional text and keeps
   everything else about the component — the number, the 96px rule, the
   tracking and the red. */
.marker { margin-block-end: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3.5rem); font-size: 14px; line-height: 17px; }
.marker span:last-child { flex: 1 1 auto; }

/* ═══ the record — facts on hairlines ═══════════════════════════════════ */
/* A record on a photograph is one line, not a stack: three facts divided
   by hairlines, sitting in the frame's floor strip. */
.facts--row {
  max-width: none !important;
  display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--s-4) 0;
  margin-block: var(--s-6) 0; padding: 0; max-width: none;
  border-block-start: 1px solid var(--rule);
  padding-block-start: var(--s-4);
}
.facts--row span { padding-inline: var(--s-5); border-inline-start: 1px solid var(--rule-fine); }
.facts--row span:first-child { padding-inline-start: 0; border-inline-start: 0; }

.facts { margin-block-start: var(--s-8); border-block-start: 1px solid var(--rule); max-width: 46ch; }
.facts > div {
  display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr auto; gap: var(--s-5);
  align-items: baseline; padding-block: var(--s-4);
  border-block-end: 1px solid var(--rule-fine);
}
.facts--plain > div { display: block; }
.shot--right .facts { max-width: 46ch; }

/* ═══ the route and the control ═════════════════════════════════════════ */
.route {
  display: inline-block; margin-block-start: var(--s-8);
  font-size: 1rem; letter-spacing: normal; text-transform: none;
  padding-block-end: var(--s-2);
  border-block-end: 1px solid var(--accent);
  color: var(--ink); text-decoration: none;
  transition: color var(--dur) var(--ease-out);
}
.route:hover { color: var(--accent); }
.route:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--ink); outline-offset: 4px; }
/* The control is ferrari.com's, measured off their own page rather than
   styled to taste: 16px at regular weight, sentence case, no tracking,
   square corners, 21px of padding and 57px tall, white on #DA291C. The
   uppercase, tracked, 14px version that stood here was mine, and it read
   as a template button rather than as theirs. */
.btn {
  display: inline-flex; align-items: center;
  min-height: 57px;
  white-space: nowrap;
  margin-block-start: var(--s-9);
  padding-inline: 21px;
  background: var(--accent); color: var(--accent-ink);
  font-size: 1rem; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: normal; text-transform: none;
  text-decoration: none; transition: background-color var(--dur) var(--ease-out);
}
.btn:hover { background: #C0241A; }
.btn:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--ink); outline-offset: 3px; }

/* ═══ the silence ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   The only screen with no photograph. It exists to give the engine its
   entrance. */
.silence {
  min-height: 62svh; display: grid; align-content: center;
  background: var(--hall);
}
.silence .display { max-width: 10ch; font-size: var(--t-hero); }

/* ═══ the sheet ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
.sheet { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--s-10) var(--s-11); }
.sheet__group + .sheet__group { margin-block-start: var(--s-9); }
.sheet__label { display: block; padding-block-end: var(--s-3); border-block-end: 1px solid var(--rule); }
.sheet__rows { margin: 0; }
/* `1fr auto` starved the label: the value took whatever it asked for and
   MAXIMUM POWER, SPECIFIC OUTPUT and WIDTH WITHOUT MIRRORS all wrapped in
   a 403px row. The label gets a floor, and values that must not break
   say so. */
.sheet__row {
  display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(10.5rem, 1fr) auto; gap: var(--s-5);
  align-items: baseline; padding-block: var(--s-3);
  border-block-end: 1px solid var(--rule-fine);
}
.sheet__row dt { margin: 0; }
/* a record ends on its content, not on a rule with nothing under it */
.sheet__rows > .sheet__row:last-child { border-block-end: 0; }
.facts > div:last-child { border-block-end: 0; }
.sheet__row dd {
  margin: 0; font-size: 1.0625rem; font-weight: 500; text-align: end;
  text-wrap: balance;
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums lining-nums; font-feature-settings: "tnum" 1, "lnum" 1;
}
@media (max-width: 60rem) { .sheet { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--s-10); } }

/* ═══ motion ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   One temporal idea: the camera arrives. A shot opens vertically out of
   the hall while the lens closes on the object, and the title settles
   after the picture does — the frame arrives, the object catches up, the
   words land last.

   Three registered properties drive it, all defaulting to their finished
   state, so a page with no script, or one asked for reduced motion, is
   the completed composition rather than an empty screen.             */
@property --open { syntax: "<number>"; inherits: true; initial-value: 0; }
@property --zoom { syntax: "<number>"; inherits: true; initial-value: 1; }
@property --t    { syntax: "<number>"; inherits: true; initial-value: 1; }
@property --par  { syntax: "<number>"; inherits: true; initial-value: 0; }

.shot { --open: 0; --zoom: 1; --t: 1; --par: 0; }
.shot__media { clip-path: inset(calc(var(--open) * 9%) 0 calc(var(--open) * 9%) 0); }
/* Type does not animate on this page — not a fade, not a rise. The
   picture moves; the words are simply there when the screen is. */

/* the still: everything arrived, nothing moving */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .shot { --open: 0 !important; --zoom: 1 !important; --t: 1 !important; --par: 0 !important; }
  .shot__type { transform: none !important; }
}

/* ═══ the phone is a different cut ══════════════════════════════════════
   A phone scroll is fast: the same travel reads as lag. The shots keep
   their full height, the camera move is halved, and the title does not
   float at all — at this size the settle is invisible and only costs
   frames. Crops are authored per shot, never inherited from desktop. */
@media (max-width: 60rem) {
  :root { --head-h-m: 120px; }
  .shot { min-height: 100svh; }

  /* A 390-wide window takes a narrow vertical slice out of a 16:9 frame,
     so the desktop crop — which pushes the subject away from the words —
     would show nothing but floor here. The phone re-centres on the car by
     default, and any shot that needs a different centre carries --pos-m. */
  .shot__media img { object-position: var(--pos-m, 50% 50%); }

  /* the type is the width of the screen, not of a desktop column: at 29vw
     it was breaking every two or three words */
  .shot__type > *,
  .shot__sub,
  .shot__type .read,
  .shot__type .facts { max-width: none; }

  /* the first screen begins under the dealer's masthead, and the block is
     bottom-set, so it is the block's own top padding that keeps the title
     out from behind the bar */
  .shot--hero .shot__type { padding-block-start: calc(var(--head-h-m) + var(--s-4)); }
  .shot--hero .shot__title { font-size: clamp(2rem, 8.6vw, 2.75rem); }
  .shot--hero .shot__sub { margin-block-start: var(--s-5); }
  .shot--hero .facts { margin-block-start: var(--s-6); }
  .shot--hero .facts > div { padding-block: var(--s-3); }
  .shot--hero .route { margin-block-start: var(--s-6); }

  /* The close carries the most type on the page — eyebrow, headline,
     paragraph, control and its note. Over the rear three-quarter's yellow
     bodywork that block measured 2.18:1, and no scrim deep enough to fix
     it would leave a photograph underneath. So the phone gets its own
     composition: the shot plays at 56svh and the words stand on the page's
     own black beneath it, where the control is also easiest to hit. */
  .shot--close { min-height: 0; display: block; }
  .shot--close .shot__media { position: relative; inset: auto; height: 56svh; }
  .shot--close .shot__lift {
    background: linear-gradient(to top, rgb(0 0 0 / .85) 0%, rgb(0 0 0 / .25) 26%, rgb(0 0 0 / 0) 46%);
  }
  .shot--close::after { display: none; }
  .shot--close .shot__type {
    padding-block: var(--s-9) var(--s-10);
    background: var(--hall);
  }
  .shot__media { inset: -5% 0; }
  .shot__type { padding-block-end: clamp(2.5rem, 7vh, 4rem); }
  .shot__title { max-width: 16ch; }
  .shot--hero .shot__title { max-width: 11ch; }
  .shot--right .shot__type { text-align: start; }
  .shot--right .shot__type .read, .shot--right .shot__type .facts { margin-inline-start: 0; }
  /* the type sits over the bottom of the picture on a phone, so the lift
     is vertical there rather than directional */
  .shot__lift, .shot--right .shot__lift {
    background: linear-gradient(to top, rgb(0 0 0 / .92) 0%, rgb(0 0 0 / .74) 30%, rgb(0 0 0 / .22) 58%, rgb(0 0 0 / 0) 78%);
  }
  .silence { min-height: 50svh; }
}

/* ═══ the dealer's navigation band pins ═════════════════════════════════
   Taken from the Purosangue build, which took it from cauleyferrari.com
   itself: the contact bar rides away with the page and the navigation band
   stays at the top. `.header` is absolute, so lifting the band out of its
   flow costs no reflow below it — and on a page made of full-screen shots
   the band is the only chrome, so it is counted in the composition rather
   than added to it. */
body.nav-pinned .header__navigation-section {
  position: fixed; inset-inline: 0; inset-block-start: 0; z-index: 10;
  /* 60% black let paragraphs and section markers read straight through the
     dealer's own name at every scroll position */
  background: #000;
}
:target, .shot, .band { scroll-margin-block-start: 96px; }
@media (max-width: 64rem) {
  /* no contact bar to lose at this width, so the whole header pins at once */
  body.nav-pinned .header { position: fixed; }
  body.nav-pinned .header__navigation-section { position: relative; }
}

/* ═══ the dealer's footer, minimally corrected ══════════════════════════
   Two lines in the retailer's own footer — the copyright credit and the
   "powered by" link — are #6f6f6f on #181818 at 11px, which measures
   3.53:1 and is under AA. They come from the dealer's live site, and the
   page ships them, so they are this page's problem too. #818181 is the
   lightest grey that clears the floor (4.56:1) and the smallest change
   that fixes it: nothing else about their footer is touched. */
.super-footer__credit,
.power a span { color: #818181; }
